A||||8||Supernaut||unibody repair questions||||5||
Z||000000||Supernaut||07-11-2000||10:21 PM||||I removed the nose and fenders to see what if any frame damage there was and this is what I saw...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/cir1.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/cir2.jpg"> <BR>Judging from the wrinkles, it took a harder lick than I had originally thought.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/arw1.jpg"> <BR>I need some advice...is there any sense in taking it to a frame shop?  Is this type of repair a lost art? (or is this why every fiero I see in the boneyard has minor front-end damage?)<P>Something else I considered was cutting the bumper and nose off as indicated by the lines below...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/cut1.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/cut2.jpg"> <BR>and having an intact piece from a parts car welded back in place?<P>I am open to and will be grateful for any suggestions.  Thanks!<P>---------------------<BR>Supernaut<BR>'86 GT (in progress)<BR>greg@pricedoor.com<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||07-12-2000||11:03 AM||||<BR>Forgive my ignorance here, but let me ask a question, how does the nose clip and fenders hang when attached to the frontend as is?  Is the damage noticeable?  Because from the pics the damage does not look too severe.<P>If the fenders and front clip were broken because of an accident, you may not have a clue how good stuff will hang.<P>Just thinking out loud here, but if you remove the front bumper, could you heat up the front end and hammer it straight?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000002||lennyb4||07-12-2000||11:09 AM||||I was just thinking the same thing. The damage does not look to bad. I would try to straighten it out myself. Just use some assorted hammers, vise grips, propane torch, and small anvils. Ive straightened worse then that before. Just take your time. Try it and if you cant get it then take it to a shop. I mean what do you have to lose? Your not going mess it up any worse.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smallPegasus.gif">  <BR>87 Coupe... Got rice???||||reg||6
Z||000003||theogre||07-12-2000||04:58 PM||||Unlike some cars, that is frame that you bent. You hit it a little over from the corner welds so you may have saved transmitting anything back toward the A post.<P>It's hard to say from the pictures, or even in person by just looking at it. You have to remember that Fiero pannels are flexible. They will hide subtle damage to the side frame or A post easily.<P>You could frame check it. Yes it's still done. A quick check, and likely cheaper, is see how a 4 wheel alignment is. If the frame is bent the alignment is likely off in strange ways. (MAke sure the alignment is printed on paper and that the tech explains it before it's adjusted. DO NOT leave the car at the shop unattended.) If the alignment is way off on that right corner, or if the tech finds any worn front end parts, run it thru the frame machine before doing anything. You may still have hidden damage in the side frame or A post that can only be checked on the frame rig, and you want to eliminate that posibility completely before doing any repairs.<P>Another quick check it to measure the distance between the side frames in various points and compair them to the same measurements on another car. It looks like you have spread the front part of the side rails out. If that's all you've done then yes your cut/weld idea will work. won't be easy but will work. You need to measure under the car as well to make sure the main bulk of the side rail is in good shape.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Supernaut||07-13-2000||10:46 PM||||Thanks for the input guys!<P>The hood was off when I got the car, broken to bits, the nose was scraped (guess it went under a bumper) but in position and the front fenders were ok.<P>I have an '84 SE sitting around...I'll start taking some measurements this weekend and making some comparisons.  And I might just drag my hammers out and try my hand at reshaping this baby.<P>Thanks again.<P>------------------<BR>Supernaut<BR>86 GT (in progress)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/signat.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000005||webbee||07-14-2000||10:19 PM||||The guy that taught me bodywork liked to use a come-a-long strapped to a telephone pole to very slowly pull things back into shape. He also used bottle jacks w/4x4 to push frame members & body panels. It beat hammering! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierodave||07-15-2000||04:19 AM||||Here is a pic of the damage to the front of the Indy I restored, Note the core support damage <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1340007&p=14687589&Sequence=1" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1340007&p=14687589&Sequence=1</A> <BR>here are some pic's of the core support removed.....you can use a spot weld cutter and drill out the spot welds on the car or do as i did,  I used a plasma cutter and cut the spot welds all out in around 20 minutes,very easy! <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795702&Sequence=3" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795702&Sequence=3</A> <BR>just take your time and look for all the spot welds <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795706&Sequence=3" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795706&Sequence=3</A> <BR>here is a pic of the support removed <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795602&Sequence=3" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795602&Sequence=3</A> <BR>here are some of the new support welded in <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795629&Sequence=3" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795629&Sequence=3</A> <BR>different angle <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795644&Sequence=3" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795644&Sequence=3</A> <BR>amother angle <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795669&Sequence=3" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=101570&a=1368386&p=14795669&Sequence=3</A> <BR>the whole job you see listed above took a total of 8 hours!  removing one from a donor car  and the wrecked support from the car, along with welding it back into the car and installing the radiator and condenser back into the car.<BR>even if you have never done it before  you can remove the wrecked and donor support with a couple drill bits and take it to a shop and have them wire feed weld it back into place for you, BEST of all!!!  It looks like there was never any damage.<BR>just ask todd (Lambo) a forum member who purchased this car from me!!<BR>thanks for the space <BR>FD<p>[This message has been edited by fierodave (edited 07-15-2000).]||||reg||1
